'Taylor Swift Toy Story 5 Song Condition: ''I''m Not in the Business of Doing That''',
'',
'Taylor Swift Toy Story 5 song condition revealed at the 1 November 2026 Q&A: Swift would only write ''I Knew It, I Knew You'' once Disney confirmed no other songwriter would lose the job — then she wrote it in eight hours.',
'',
$$<p>The Taylor Swift Toy Story 5 song condition is now the most quoted line of her awards-season campaign. At a special screening and question-and-answer session in New York City on 1 November 2026, Swift told director Andrew Stanton that she would only write the film's end-credits song, "I Knew It, I Knew You", once she was certain no other songwriter would lose the job because of her. <a href="https://parade.com/news/taylor-swift-reveals-the-one-condition-she-set-to-write-the-closing-song-for-toy-story-5">Parade reported the revelation on 4 November 2026</a>; JustJared, Geo and EntertainmentNow reported the same exchange on 3 November 2026.</p>
<h2>At a glance: Taylor Swift Toy Story 5 song condition</h2>
<table>
<tr><th>Song</th><td>"I Knew It, I Knew You", the end-credits song for Toy Story 5</td></tr>
<tr><th>Revealed at</th><td>Q&A, New York City, 1 November 2026, with Andrew Stanton</td></tr>
<tr><th>Swift's condition</th><td>No other songwriter to be pushed off the project for her to take it</td></tr>
<tr><th>Her words</th><td>"I'm not in the business of doing that"</td></tr>
<tr><th>Written in</th><td>Eight hours, with Jack Antonoff, after an early advance screening</td></tr>
<tr><th>Inspiration</th><td>Jessie, voiced by Joan Cusack, and female friendship</td></tr>
<tr><th>Chart result</th><td>Swift's 15th number one on the Billboard Hot 100, Parade reported</td></tr>
<tr><th>Awards season</th><td>HMMA nomination, announced 2 November 2026; Oscar campaign under way</td></tr>
</table>
<h2>Taylor Swift Toy Story 5 song: the condition in her own words</h2>
<p>Swift was, by her own account, repeatedly blunt with Disney about the delay. She had taken a couple of months to come back to the project, and she wanted an honest answer. "I was repeatedly asking, 'I know that it took me a couple months to come back to this project. Be honest: did you guys replace me?'" she said at the Q&A, Parade reported.</p>
<p>"I don't want to kick somebody off of this project that you reached out to after [me] because I understand, but I'm not in the business of doing that," Swift said, according to Parade. JustJared, Geo and EntertainmentNow reported the same line. Fans have seized on the final clause in particular.</p>
<p>Stanton's answer removed the obstacle. Swift told him she had discovered there was no other person on the film's mood board for the song, Parade reported. "Everybody said, 'No, we just decided there wasn't going to be an end credit song [if you didn't do it],'" she said to Stanton. JustJared reported Stanton's version in similar terms: Swift was the only person he wanted, and "nothing else made sense".</p>
<h2>Why Stanton waited, and why there was no backup plan</h2>
<p>The reason there was no replacement waiting in the wings lies in the direction Stanton wanted for the film's music. Randy Newman has composed music for the Toy Story franchise since the original film in 1995, and Stanton wanted a different direction for the fifth film, The Express Tribune reported. JustJared reported the same point: Stanton wanted a change for this instalment, and Swift was his preferred choice from the start.</p>
<p>That preference explains the long pause. Swift was initially unavailable to discuss the project, and Stanton believed the film might simply have no original end-credits song rather than hand the job to someone else, The Express Tribune reported. A producer on the film also wrote Swift a personal letter asking her to contribute, JustJared reported: a single-artist pursuit, not an open call.</p>
<h2>Eight hours, Jessie, and a childhood dream</h2>
<p>Once the condition was settled, the song itself arrived fast. After attending an early advance screening, Swift wrote, produced and recorded "I Knew It, I Knew You" within eight hours with her long-time collaborator Jack Antonoff, Parade reported. EntertainmentNow reported the same sequence.</p>
<p>Parade reported that Swift drew inspiration from Jessie, voiced by Joan Cusack. "When I saw this brilliant film that Andrew made, I realized exactly why he knew I would love it," Swift explained, according to Parade. "It's centered around Jessie, who's my favorite character, and it deals with female friendship." The connection is personal: Swift has wanted to write for the franchise since watching the first Toy Story in 1995 at the age of five, Parade reported.</p>
<h2>What the song has already done</h2>
<p>The commercial result is already in the record books. "I Knew It, I Knew You" became Swift's 15th number one song on the Billboard Hot 100, Parade reported, leaving only The Beatles (20), Mariah Carey (19) and Elvis Presley (18) ahead of her on that all-time count. The song she almost did not write, because she refused to displace anyone, became one of the defining hits of her career.</p>
<p>The awards season has followed the same path. Swift's Toy Story 5 song was nominated for a Hollywood Music in Media Award in the Song for an Animated Film category, as reported in <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/taylor-swift-hollywood-music-media-awards-nomination-20261103">Growentix's report on the nomination, announced on 2 November 2026</a>. The 1 November screening was part of that campaign: <a href="https://www.realitytea.com/2026/11/04/taylor-swift-oscar-campaign-toy-story-5-screening/">Reality Tea reported on 4 November 2026</a> that Swift stayed to greet attendees, posed for selfies and signed autographs, and is seeking her first Academy Award nomination in the Best Original Song category, with nominations expected in January 2027. The same screening also produced <a href="https://growentix.cloud/articles/taylor-swift-napoleon-dynamite-toy-story-5-screening-20261102">Growentix's report on the Napoleon Dynamite voices at the Toy Story 5 screening</a>.</p>
<h2>What to watch next</h2>
<p>Three things are worth watching from here. The first is the Oscar race: whether "I Knew It, I Knew You" converts its campaign into a Best Original Song nomination when nominations are announced in January 2027, as Reality Tea reported. A nomination would be Swift's first Academy Award nomination. The second is the Hollywood Music in Media Awards ceremony, where the song is already nominated. The third is simpler: whether Swift's line about not being "in the business of doing that" becomes a lasting statement of how she works. On the evidence of this week, it already has.</p>
<p>For now, the story stands as Swift told it: months to answer, repeated questions about whether she had been replaced, a refusal to push someone else off a project, and then a number one song in eight hours about Jessie and female friendship.</p>$$,
